@item @option{-r} @tab Recovery mode: restore files from @file{.ESV} backups
created after a crash.
@item @option{-mb} @tab Treat the next file as a binary file.
(Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-mn} @tab Treat the next file as an ASCII (text) file.
This is the default.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-@var{n}c} @tab Set the maximum number of columns to @var{n}
(e.g.@: @option{-80c}).  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-@var{n}u} @tab Set the maximum number of undo steps to @var{n}
(e.g.@: @option{-100u}).  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-@var{n}m} @tab Set the number of editing steps before an
auto-save is performed (e.g.@: @option{-50m}).  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-k @var{password}} @tab Decode the next file with @var{password}
for reading and encode it for writing.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-pm} @tab Treat the next file as a compiler message file.
@item @option{-pt} @tab Interpret message file for ``traditional'' compiler
output (see Compiler Options).  (Documented since v1.4, German manpage.)
@item @option{-pe} @tab Interpret message file for ``extended'' compiler
output (see Compiler Options).  (Documented since v1.4, German manpage.)
@item @option{-display @var{dpy}} @tab Connect to X server @var{dpy} (X11 only).
@item @option{-fn @var{f}} @tab Synonym for @option{-font}.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-font @var{f}} @tab Use font @var{f} for text (X11 only;
default: @code{8x13}).
@item @option{-g @var{g}} @tab Synonym for @option{-geometry}.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@item @option{-geometry @var{g}} @tab Set window size/position (X11 only;
minimum 80x25; e.g.@: @option{-g 80x20+4+4}).
@item @option{-iconic} @tab Start minimised (X11 only).
@item @option{-pcmap} @tab Use a private colourmap (X11 only).  For a
palette (8-bit) display whose shared colourmap other programs have filled:
the editor's 16 colours then get cells of their own.  With or without it,
the editor paints a palette display in its own 16 colours.  Without it,
when the shared colourmap is full the editor takes, for each colour it
cannot get, the nearest colour already there if one is close, and
otherwise moves to a private colourmap by itself and says so once on the
terminal it was started from; it never stops for want of colours.
@end multitable

@section Key cheatsheet

@subsection Key bindings

@cindex key bindings
@cindex key styles
@cindex Borland key style
Every key below is a row of the editor's key table, in the Borland style
(the default) and in the CUA style (@samp{Options @result{} Editor
@result{} Keys}).  The same rows name the keys on the bottom bar and in
the menus, and this section is generated from them, so a key named
anywhere in the editor is a key that works.  Whether a key reaches xwpe
at all depends on the terminal; the next subsection says which ones.

@c The tables below are generated from we_bindings.c by tools/keymap-texi.
@include chapters/keys-generated.texi

A few notes on the tables.  @kbd{Alt-F9}, @kbd{Alt-F8} and @kbd{Alt-F7}
(Compile, Next error, Previous error) work where they reach the editor
(the X11 and Wayland windows, the Linux console); terminal emulators keep
them, so the menus show only the @kbd{Alt}-letter keys.  In the
programming editor @kbd{Alt-G} opens the assistant instead of Go to line,
so Go to line is reached from the Search menu there and the menu does not
offer the key it cannot honour.  @kbd{Alt-F5} (User screen) replays the
program's own full screen, for programs that paint (ANSI colour, cursor
positioning): the console (wpe) hands the terminal back, and xwpe (X11 and
Wayland) paints it in its own window.  @kbd{Ctrl-G P} focuses the Messages
panel with the program's captured output (both backends).  @kbd{Ctrl-G O}
selects the debugger (gdb, jdb, sdb, dbx).

@subsection Whether a key reaches xwpe
@cindex function keys, macOS
@cindex function keys, GNOME Terminal
@cindex Option key as Meta

A key does the same thing in xwpe on Linux and on macOS: the terminal
sends the same bytes for it on both, and xwpe reads them the same way.
In the Borland key style @key{F1} is Help, @key{F2} Save, @key{F3} Open
(the file manager) and @key{F4} Search, or Go to cursor while a debug
session runs.  What can
differ is whether the key press reaches the terminal at all, and that is
decided by the keyboard and the system before xwpe sees anything.

The terminal, X11 and Wayland read keys through one table, so the same
key does the same thing on all three.  A terminal sends a Ctrl or Alt
function key as an escape sequence, and xwpe reads it whether or not the
terminal's terminfo entry names it: @kbd{Ctrl-F8} is
@code{ESC [ 19;5~} under xterm and under @env{TERM=linux} alike.  The
same holds for @kbd{Shift} and @kbd{Ctrl} with the cursor keys:
@kbd{Shift-Right} (@code{ESC [ 1;2C}) marks a block and
@kbd{Ctrl-Right} (@code{ESC [ 1;5C}) moves a word under
@env{TERM=xterm-256color}, @env{TERM=tmux-256color},
@env{TERM=screen-256color}, @env{TERM=st-256color} and @env{TERM=linux}
alike.  rxvt-unicode spells them its own way (@kbd{Shift-Up} is
@code{ESC [ a}, @kbd{Ctrl-Right} @code{ESC O c}, @kbd{Ctrl-End}
@code{ESC [ 8 ^}) and they work there too.  The
Linux console has no Ctrl or Alt function keys and sends
@kbd{Shift-F1} to @kbd{Shift-F10} as @key{F11} to @key{F20}; xwpe reads
the shift state the console reports and takes them as Shift-@key{F1} to
Shift-@key{F10} there only.  @kbd{Alt-Left} and @kbd{Alt-Right} move by
word like @kbd{Ctrl-Left} and @kbd{Ctrl-Right}, and @kbd{Alt-Home} and
@kbd{Alt-End} jump like @kbd{Ctrl-Home} and @kbd{Ctrl-End}, in all
three.  @key{F11} and @key{F12} are keys on all three, with Shift, Ctrl and
Alt, but the Borland key set gives them no action (under X they
share their key symbols with the Sun keyboard's Stop and Again keys, which
are not read as those).  A terminal sends @kbd{Alt-}@var{key}
as @key{Esc} and the key together, and xwpe joins them when the second
byte follows within about 50 milliseconds.

@cindex modifyOtherKeys
@cindex kitty keyboard protocol
@cindex Ctrl-Shift, in a terminal
A legacy terminal cannot tell @kbd{Ctrl-Shift-P} from @kbd{Ctrl-P},
@kbd{Ctrl-H} from @key{Backspace}, @kbd{Ctrl-I} from @key{Tab},
@kbd{Ctrl-M} from @key{Return} or @kbd{Ctrl-[} from @key{Esc}, and waits
to see whether a key follows an @key{Esc}.  xwpe therefore asks the
terminal for xterm's modifyOtherKeys (@code{ESC [ > 4 ; 2 m}) and the kitty
keyboard protocol (@code{ESC [ > 5 u}), and gives them back (@code{ESC [ < u},
@code{ESC [ > 4 m}) when it ends, is stopped or crashes, and whenever it
hands the terminal to a program.  A terminal that knows either sends each of
these chords as a report (@code{ESC [ 112 ; 6 u} for @kbd{Ctrl-Shift-P},
@code{ESC [ 27 ; 6 ; 112 ~} in the xterm form), which xwpe reads as the key
it is.  A terminal that knows neither keeps sending the legacy bytes.  The
Linux console is sent nothing.  tmux passes the reports only with
@code{set -g extended-keys on}, and GNU screen passes neither.

@table @asis
@item Linux (xterm, Konsole, the console)
@key{F1}--@key{F4} always arrive.  One known exception: GNOME Terminal
keeps @key{F1} for its own help (and @key{F10} for its menu, @key{F11}
for full screen) by default; turn those off in its preferences, under
Shortcuts.

@item macOS, as shipped
The physical @key{F1} lowers the screen brightness, @key{F2} raises it,
@key{F3} opens Mission Control and @key{F4} opens Spotlight or Launchpad,
depending on the keyboard.  xwpe receives nothing.

@item macOS, holding @key{fn}, or with the standard function keys turned on
With @key{fn} held, or with @emph{System Settings -> Keyboard -> ``Use F1,
F2, etc. keys as standard function keys''} ticked, Terminal.app and iTerm2
send the same bytes as a Linux terminal and xwpe behaves exactly the same:
@key{F4} searches, or runs to the cursor inside a session.
@end table

Three more things macOS keeps for itself by default, each with its way out:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@kbd{Ctrl-F2} to @kbd{Ctrl-F8} are documented by Apple as keyboard-focus
shortcuts (menu bar, Dock, window, toolbar@dots{}).  When they are on, the
debugger's @kbd{Ctrl-F2} (quit), @kbd{Ctrl-F7} (watch) and @kbd{Ctrl-F8}
(breakpoint) never arrive.  Turn them off in @emph{System Settings ->
Keyboard -> Keyboard Shortcuts -> Keyboard}, or use the @kbd{Ctrl-G}
letters below.

@item
@kbd{Ctrl-Left} and @kbd{Ctrl-Right} switch desktops (Spaces), and
@key{F11} shows the desktop.

@item
The Option key types special characters instead of acting as @kbd{Alt},
so @kbd{Alt-F}, @kbd{Alt-X} and the other menu keys do nothing until it is
set to act as Meta: in Terminal.app, @emph{Settings -> Profiles ->
Keyboard -> ``Use Option as Meta key''}; in iTerm2, @emph{Settings ->
Profiles -> Keys -> Left Option key: Esc+}.  Pressing @key{Esc} and then
the letter by hand is not the same: xwpe joins the two only when they
arrive within a few milliseconds, as a terminal sends them for
@kbd{Alt-}@var{letter}.  Until Option acts as Meta, open the menus with
the mouse, or with @key{F10} while holding @key{fn}.
@end itemize

Terminal.app and iTerm2, as shipped, do not pass the Command key to a
program running in them.

@subsection Without function keys

Every essential command also has a key that is not a function key.  The
@kbd{Ctrl-} chords work in any terminal on both systems as shipped; the
@kbd{Alt-} keys need Option set to act as Meta on macOS (see above).

