@subsection Clipboard keys outside a terminal

@kbd{Alt-Delete} copies the marked text to the system selection on X11 and
Wayland, and to the editor's clipboard in a terminal (as @kbd{Ctrl-C}).
@kbd{Alt-Insert} pastes what another application copied on X11 and Wayland,
and the editor's clipboard otherwise (as @kbd{Ctrl-V}).

@subsection Quitting

@table @kbd
@item Alt-X
Quit from xwpe in the Borland style (@kbd{Alt-F4} in the CUA style).  If
files have unsaved changes, xwpe prompts for each: @samp{Yes} to save,
@samp{No} to discard, @key{ESC} to cancel the quit (no further files are
closed).  When a file cannot be written, the error is shown and the quit
stops there, with that window still open.  (Documented since v1.4.)
@end table

@section Common User Access (CUA) keybindings

@cindex CUA
@cindex Common User Access
@cindex keybinding variant
@cindex OSF Motif

xwpe supports a second keybinding set compatible with OSF Motif and
MS-Windows conventions (Common User Access).  Select it in
@samp{Options @result{} Editor @result{} Keys @result{} CUA}.  The
tables of the key bindings above give every key in both styles, side by
side, so a key that differs between them is a row whose two columns
differ.  (Documented since v1.4.)

@section Alt and ESC on non-X11 terminals

@cindex Alt key
@cindex ESC key
@cindex terminal Alt

On a character terminal (without X11), @kbd{Alt-X} is entered by pressing
@key{ESC} then @kbd{X}.  A bare @key{ESC} is entered by pressing
@key{ESC} followed by @key{Return}.  (Documented since v1.4.)

@section Special windows

@cindex windows
@cindex special windows

This section describes the special-purpose windows in xwpe.  All are
read-only unless otherwise noted.  (Documented since v1.4.)

@subsection The Find Window

@cindex Find Window

The Find Window lists all directories containing files that match the
search criteria from @samp{File @result{} Find}.  Press @key{Return}
or @kbd{Alt-S} to open a file manager for the selected directory
showing the matching files.  Press @key{ESC} to close the window.

@subsection The Grep Window

@cindex Grep Window

The Grep Window lists all files containing strings that match the
search criteria from @samp{File @result{} Grep}.  Press @key{Return}
or @kbd{Alt-S} to open the marked file for editing; the cursor jumps
to the first occurrence.  Press @key{ESC} to close the window.

When nothing matched, the Find and Grep windows are empty: @key{Return}
and @kbd{Alt-S} then say @samp{No entry in this list} and the window
stays open.  The same holds in a Function Index section with no manual
pages and in a project file list whose last file was deleted.

@subsection The Message Window

@cindex Message Window

Compiler and linker errors are listed in the Message Window (read-only).
Select an error by moving with cursor keys and pressing @key{Return},
or click with the mouse.  The source file is switched to the current
window (if not already loaded, it is opened in a new window) and the
cursor jumps to the position indicated by the compiler.
(Detail from German manpage.)

@subsection The Project Window

@cindex Project Window

Displays all files in the project.  @kbd{Alt-A} or @key{Insert} opens
the window in add-status to add a file.  @kbd{Alt-E} or @key{Return}
edits an entry.  @kbd{Alt-D} or @key{Delete} deletes an item (only
available when the Project Window is open).
(Detail from German manpage.)  Press @kbd{Alt-E} or @key{Return} to edit an entry,
@kbd{Alt-D} or @key{Delete} to remove an entry, @kbd{Alt-O} to open
project options, @key{ESC} to close.

@subsection The Variable Window

@cindex Variable Window

The Variable Window (opened from Project Options) displays project
variables.  Press @kbd{Alt-A} or @key{Insert} to add, @kbd{Alt-E}
or @key{Return} to edit, @kbd{Alt-D} or @key{Delete} to remove,
@key{ESC} to close.  (Documented since v1.4.)

@subsection The Install Window

@cindex Install Window

The Install Window (opened from Project Options) displays and edits
the commands executed by the @samp{install} target.  Press @kbd{Alt-A}
or @key{Insert} to add, @kbd{Alt-E} or @key{Return} to edit,
@kbd{Alt-D} or @key{Delete} to remove, @key{ESC} to close.
(Documented since v1.4.)

@subsection The Watch Window

@cindex Watch Window

The Watch Window shows the values of variables selected via the watch
function (@pxref{Debugging}).  Use cursor keys and the search function
to navigate.  Press @key{Return} to edit a selected variable, or to
add a new one if the last line is selected.  Press @key{Insert} to
insert a new entry; @key{Delete} to remove one.  If a variable is not
found in the current scope, a warning is displayed.

@subsection The Stack Window

@cindex Stack Window

The Stack Window shows the program call stack (read-only).  Navigate
with cursor keys; press @key{Return} to jump to a stack frame in the
source.  The stack can be examined when a signal is caught by the
program.

@section The Execution Manager

@cindex Execution Manager

The Execution Manager (@samp{File @result{} Execute}) selects and runs
executable files and shell scripts.  Only files with the execute permission
are shown in the Files list.  (Documented since v1.4.)

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@headitem Key @tab Action
@item @kbd{Alt-N} @tab Select the Name line.  Enter a file name or mask.
@item @kbd{Alt-F} @tab Select the Files list.
@item @kbd{Alt-D} @tab Select the Directory line.
@item @kbd{Alt-T} @tab Select the directory tree.
@item @kbd{Alt-C} @tab Change to the entered directory.
@item @kbd{Alt-E} @tab Execute the selected file.
@item @key{ESC} @tab Close the execution manager.
@end multitable

@section Function Index

@cindex Function Index
@cindex man pages
@cindex MANPATH

The Function Index (@samp{Help @result{} Function Index} or @kbd{Ctrl-F1})
creates a list of all manual pages found via the @env{MANPATH} environment
variable.  The index is divided into sections.
(Documented since v1.4.)

@table @strong
@item User Commands
Section 1 man pages.
@item System Calls
C system call functions (section 2).
@item C Library Functions
C library functions (section 3).
@item Devices & Network Interfaces
Section 4.
@item File Formats
Section 5.
@item Games & Demos
Section 6.
@item Environments, Tables and TROFF Macros
Section 7.
@item Maintenance Commands
System administration commands (section 8).
@end table

Select a section to see a list of available man pages.  Select a page
by pressing @key{Return} or clicking on the name.  Man pages are
read-only documents but can serve as sources for editing commands.

A manual page can also be viewed by name via @samp{Help @result{}
Function} (@kbd{Alt-H T}).

@section C programming utilities

@cindex C programming
@cindex show definition
@cindex matching bracket
@cindex beautify

For C and C++ source files, xwpe provides several programming aids
accessed from the @samp{Run} menu or via @kbd{Ctrl-O} chords.
(Documented since v1.4.)

@subsection Show Definition (@kbd{Ctrl-O S})

Jump to the definition of a C expression.  xwpe opens the file
containing the definition and highlights it.  Searchable definitions
include: @code{struct}, @code{union}, @code{class}, @code{typedef},
preprocessor @code{#define}, @code{extern} variables, and functions.

If a project is defined, all project files are scanned.  All
@code{#include}d files are scanned recursively, following the
System-Include-Path defined in @samp{Options @result{} Programming}.

If the name has multiple definitions (e.g.@: both a @code{struct} and
an @code{extern} variable), use @kbd{Ctrl-O N} (Show Next Definition)
to cycle through them.

@subsection Matching Bracket (@kbd{Ctrl-O K})

If the cursor is on a bracket, xwpe finds the matching opening or
closing bracket and moves the cursor to it.  If the cursor is not on a
bracket, xwpe moves to the next opening bracket.  Recognised bracket
pairs: @code{@{@}}, @code{[]}, and @code{()}.

@subsection Beautify (@kbd{Ctrl-O B})

Reformat C source code by re-indenting all blocks according to their
nesting depth.  The indentation step size is taken from the auto-indent
setting in @samp{Options @result{} Editor}.

@section Project options dialog

@cindex project options

The project options dialog (@samp{Project @result{} Options},
@kbd{Alt-P O}) provides the following fields.
(Documented since v1.4.)

@table @strong
@item Compiler
The path to the compiler.
@item Compiler-Options
Flags passed to the compiler.
@item Loader-Options
Flags passed to the linker.
@item Executable-Name
The name of the resulting executable (default: @code{a.out}).
@item Store Objects in Library
If a filename is entered, all object files are stored in this library,
which is then used automatically for linking.
@item Compiler-Style
@code{GNU} (default) or @code{Other} (custom message-string).
@item Variables ...
Opens the Variable Window to define and manage project variables.
@item Install ...
Opens the Install Window to define and manage install commands.
@item Save
Save the project file.
@item Save As
Save the project file under a new name.
@item CANCEL
Close without saving.
@end table
